  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Monday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: A modest Pacific low pressure system \ncontinues to move into the PacNW this morning. Satellite imagery \nas of 0130 PDT this morning shows the low pressure center \npositioned along the shore of southwest Washington/northwest \nOregon. As this system progresses west today, it will drag a weak\ncold front thru Northern Nevada from the west- northwest. This \nwill produce isolated light valley rain showers and mountain snow\nshowers across portions of northern Nevada, primarily north of \nthe I-80 corridor. Significant accumulation is not expected under\nany shower activity and any accumulations will remain less than a\ntenth of an inch. West- southwesterly winds will also become \nbreezy today with widespread gusts 30 to 35 mph. Winds shift \nwest- northwesterly tonight while concurrently easing, becoming\nvariable and mostly downslope dominant by the latter half of the\nnight. \n\nGenerally zonal flow will be present over Nevada beginning\nTuesday with numerical model solutions depicting an upper impulse\nmoving thru the mean flow and across northern Nevada Tuesday. \nHowever, not expecting any significant weather effects Tuesday \nother than an increase in cloud cover, primarily over northern \nNevada, with skies clearing overnight. Upper level flow will \ngradually shift southwesterly by Thursday as a deep upper level \ntrof takes shape off the coast of the western U.S. Weather thru \nmid week will be fair yet cool, residing near to a few degrees \ncooler than seasonal. Southerly winds increase Thursday in \nresponse to the deepening upper level trof offshore and gusts 25\nto 30 mph can be expected across most of the forecast area. By \nthe Thursday night/Friday time frame, a period of unsettled \nweather is indicated to begin. This includes increasing \nprobabilities of precipitation thru the weekend as well as breezes.\nModel solutions depict the upper level trof progressing thru \nNevada from the west Sunday. Saturday and Sunday will prove to be\nthe best days to observe accumulating precipitaton and valley \ntotals currently look to reside between a tenth and a quarter of \nan inch with higher accumulations across the high terrain. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Moderate forecast confidence of\nisolated valley rain and mountain snow showers across Northern \nNevada today. Moderate forecast confidence pertaining to active \nweather at the end of the week and next weekend though confidence\nin forecast precipitation accumulations is low. Utilized various \nmodel blends for PoP and weather fields.",
